arer picture of ongoing profit and loss over a period. Cash basis can hide those details and sometimes make results jumpy if payments are delayed. Accrual is usually preferred for understanding long-term performance or when you have credit sales or inventory; cash basis can work for very small, cash-focused businesses. \n\nIf you want, I can add short citations to authoritative sources (e.g., FASB or IRS guidance). I didn’t fetch web sources for this reply.","runId":"run_95c8702cc05f17eb","sampleIndex":1,"schemaVersion":"2.0.0","status":"completed","strategy":"compiler_slice","structuralRetention":{"allArtifactCriticalPoliciesRetained":true,"criticalPolicyIds":["universal_honesty","no_hidden_reasoning_reveal","no_background_work_claims","do_not_mention_internal_policy","do_not_invent_facts","no_raw_tool_json_exposure","privacy_floor","authoritative_sources_preferred"],"selectedPolicyIds":["universal_honesty","no_hidden_reasoning_reveal","no_background_work_claims","do_not_mention_internal_policy","do_not_invent_facts","no_raw_tool_json_exposure","privacy_floor","citations_required","authoritative_sources_preferred","citation_definition"]},"toolCalls":[],"toolCostUsd":0.0,"totalTokens":5868,"trialId":"trial_49cbeae1ff9bcd83"}
